WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Orb 3D, the fate of the universe is in your hands.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:29.300
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:30.480
I'm Mike.

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:31.960
And I'm Sean.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.600
Sean, Orb 3D, are you ready for a 3D video game on the NES?

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:40.240
I'm ready to ponder some orbs.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.320
We actually have had 3D video games on the NES already.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.920
And we've also had 3D video games on the NES that actually had 3D technology in them.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.660
So Orb 3D is coming a little late to the party.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.440
It's not introducing anything new here.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:58.340
But it did come with its own set of 3D glasses.

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:05.640
And I feel like those 3D glasses, if you still have them, have to be the most valuable thing about this game.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:17.760
Yeah, I was, when I was reading the manual, because this looked high concept enough to warrant it, and it described the glasses you were supposed to get.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.080
I was kind of worried that like, oh, am I going to be able to play this game?

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.480
Like, is it going to be in stereoscopic, like red and blue stuff?

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.800
And then they're like, oh, if you don't have it, you can still play it.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:34.220
I'm like, okay. And then I played the game, and I'm like, I don't even know how the glasses would work.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:41.520
Cool. Yeah, I had the same thoughts, because I'm looking at it, and first off, the glasses are referred to as Greenovision glasses.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.240
Yeah. With like, capital G, capital V.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:48.660
So clearly, somebody trademarked this thing, and like, was ready to put it.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.180
But it looks to me like it was just those normal red and blue glasses or whatever.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:02:03.960
But the game doesn't play like that, so I couldn't find an actual picture of Greenovision glasses, because it doesn't have any strobing or red and blue effect on it.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:15.120
So the way that it describes in the manual was that you just have to like, look past the screen, and you will see objects that move, you know, like, is it like 3DS technology, where like, it makes it look like?

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:16.700
Yeah, that's what it sounded like, yeah.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.320
Like, there's no way, though, right?

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:31.420
But now I'm starting to think that it's kind of just bullshit, and you were supposed to put the glasses on, and then you would just get some placebo effect, because there's different colors.

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.460
Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:39.500
Like, you put, you know, you put the glasses on, put your hand over your face, and then someone smacks your face.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.320
And then I can't believe you thought that was going to work.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.380
Yes, yes. That's kind of what's going on here.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:59.860
This game originally released as, what was the name, Beyond the Black Hole, and it was made by the Software Toolworks, which sounds incredibly boring, because they made mostly incredibly boring games,

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.120
like Chess Master and Cribbage King.

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:05.980
Ooh, Cribbage King.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.020
Yeah, Cribbage King. They did the whole Chess Master series, though.

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.380
If you recall, we did play Chess Master on the NES.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:12.440
That was also them.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:19.300
Indeed. But, like, Mavis Beacon teaches typing.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.320
That's them.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:26.320
They made some Mario fun games, like Mario's Fun with Numbers and Mario's Preschool Fun.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:28.120
These are just, like, educational games.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:29.720
So they make educational games.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:33.160
Is Orb 3D educational at all?

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:35.600
Like, is it teaching you about 3D?

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.960
That's not something you need to learn, right?

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:44.440
It is not educational, but I think it still sort of fits into this mold that you kind of,

00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:54.780
the picture you painted of them as more of, like, interested in the tech of video entertainment more so than video games.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.100
And I think that tracks with what we got here.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:04.060
And in Orb 3D, you control a floating orb.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:07.580
You control pong paddles.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:08.860
Oh, yeah. Sorry, sorry.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:21.280
You are trying to get a floating orb to hit objects, collide with them, so that you can destroy all objects on the screen and progress to the next stage.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:28.700
You have these little paddles on the side of the screen that you, you know, that's what you really control.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:40.120
And they kind of direct the movement of the orb in, like, some kind of gravity fashion where, like, they pull the direction of the orb towards them.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:47.600
They kind of act like the paddles in Breakout where, like, you want to hit the, you want to have the paddle hit the ball on a specific curve,

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.980
so this way it hits a specific area of the other part of the screen that it's flying towards.

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.420
That's very confusing to explain.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:05:00.260
We're going to do our best over the course of the next three hours to explain how Orb 3D works to you all.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:03.180
But that's really all you need, right, Sean?

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:13.440
Like, you just need the concept of a floating orb has to hit objects, and you have these magnetic paddles on the side that kind of help guide the ball.

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:17.700
You don't need a story, but the game decides to give you one anyway.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.560
Yeah, and I don't get it.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:21.560
I don't understand why.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:31.420
It's about wizards and stuff, but everything else about, like, the visual language of this game is very sci-fi or at least futuristic in some way.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:34.800
So, yeah, I don't understand, but we got it.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:47.260
Do you know the story? You're a space pilot who's been tasked with destroying, as Sean mentioned, an evil wizard named Kron who rules the galaxy from the Ortris Orbza Zerk.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.520
Ah, yes, Orbza Zerk.

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.500
Orbza Zerk.

00:05:51.000 --> 00:05:57.480
In order to defeat the wizard, though, you can't just merely get to his fortress.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:05.660
You have to get through 30 other, I don't know, like, are we treating these like they're solar systems or something?

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:08.300
Like, they're, what are they?

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:09.580
Like, fields?

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.200
Like, 30 space fields?

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:12.860
They're just fields. They're fields, yes.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:19.600
Okay, which is actually each, like, a room, you know, a level of itself that is a puzzle.

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:31.340
You know, the first stage you can kind of just hit the objects in any particular order and clear it, but as it's made abundantly clear in the second level and, you know, really turns the game on its head,

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:42.940
every stage is kind of unique in how you clear the objects and you can't just say, oh, well, I just have to hit everything on the screen and then they'll all disappear and I'll get a new level.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:44.240
That's not how it works.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.760
I will, I guess, set it up with the second level because I think that's the most interesting one to get across the idea of, like, how these puzzles can be different.

00:06:52.000 --> 00:07:01.040
In the first stage, you are, you know, literally just dragging this orb through the power of your paddles to hit objects across a 3D space.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:02.640
Yeah, get it through some hoops.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.560
Yeah, exactly.

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:17.700
In the second puzzle, it's now pins from a pinball alley and you only get, yeah, you only get the two chances to knock down all 10 pins.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:24.080
If you don't, the frame resets and you have all 10 pins again and you must knock down all 10.

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:27.080
Fine, I'll just keep trying over and over again, right?

00:07:27.360 --> 00:07:29.780
Well, you have limited energy.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:38.340
There is a fuel system on the right-hand side of the screen that's just a bar and it's completely filled at the beginning of a level and it slowly ticks down.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:46.520
If you manage to not catch your orbs with the paddles that are on the side of the screen, then that caused extra fuel.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.440
And once you run out of fuel, it's game over.

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:59.320
So, unlike any NES game that I've talked about so far, I am going through great lengths to explain the concept of this game.

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:03.860
And I still feel like anybody who's listening would have no idea what to picture in their head.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:06.560
If they didn't play the game.

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:07.620
Do you get that feeling?

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:09.840
No, yeah, I get that feeling.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:11.680
I'm trying to think of...

00:08:11.680 --> 00:08:14.000
It's kind of like WarioWare.

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:19.000
Except you're kind of doing the same thing in every one.

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.120
Like, the rules of the game change in every level.

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.500
But the way that you're interacting with it stays the same.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:44.140
Like, you're always just sort of hitting this orb back and forth between the two paddles with the other, like, actual control method being, like, sort of calling it back to you through, like, Z-space.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.800
And everything else is just...

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.580
It's a new game, different rules.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.820
But, yeah, you're just using those same methods of control.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:59.880
So, here's a take-two, more concise read on everything I've said so far.

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:08.340
It's a top-down point of view of the space field where you can see the orb, the targets, and the paddles, which are on the screen's edges.

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:15.680
You bounce the orb into the targets on the screen, sort of like, you know, breakout meets pinball or something.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:17.280
Because you want to hit the bumpers.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:24.300
You use the D-pad to control the paddles to curve, angle, deflect, whatever.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:32.160
Adjust the depth and trajectory of the orb because you can make it, like, swing around because it's kind of constantly moving.

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:35.180
And each room has a different thing.

00:09:35.340 --> 00:09:36.340
Like, one is...

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:38.000
One's targets. One's switches.

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:39.200
One's warp holes.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.320
One's pins. One's ducks.

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:43.760
You never know. One's a bingo card.

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:53.160
One's, uh, you're trying to play, like, a golf like you would with, uh, with, like, throwing darts.

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:58.140
But instead, it's, like, you're rolling dice and you have to hit the dice on the right number.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:01.300
And, yeah, everything is different.

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:03.460
It's really weird.

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.060
So, how long did it take you to realize how to aim in 3D space?

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:12.840
Like, obviously, you, you know, you play around with the D-pad, you understand what does what.

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:16.420
But how long did it take you to get good at hitting objects?

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:19.300
I wouldn't say that I was ever very good.

00:10:19.340 --> 00:10:25.520
But it only took me a few back and forths to understand what I was supposed to do.

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:31.040
Like, you can kind of loop it around in the middle of the field for a while.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:38.680
You could play this game and not really end up using your paddles all that much if you know what you're doing.

00:10:38.840 --> 00:10:47.920
But I didn't always know what I was doing because maybe I needed those 3D glasses because your depth perception is very important here.

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:55.220
Yeah. If this game was ported to, you know, like, VR or something, like, would it finally make sense?

00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:01.340
Like, would it click better of, like, where you are in space with the orb and the objects?

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:04.020
Or is it more just, like, a controls issue?

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:06.860
I think it's more so just your perspective.

00:11:07.080 --> 00:11:11.880
Like, and just to bring it back to a more period-appropriate piece of hardware.

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:17.220
Because I think if you ported this to, like, modern VR, it would be, like, who would want to play that?

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.520
But if it was a Virtual Boy game, maybe?

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:27.240
Like, I think that that actually would be what this game would be best on.

00:11:28.460 --> 00:11:30.680
I don't know if you agreed.

00:11:30.960 --> 00:11:36.640
Yeah, I would agree with that because, if you recall, when we played on the Virtual Boy, we played Mario Tennis.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:40.480
And that got the depth of a tennis ball pretty well.

00:11:40.480 --> 00:11:47.200
It was a little hard to know when to swing, but we were able to understand, like, hey, that tennis ball is pretty far away.

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:48.320
And now it's coming at me.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.460
I think that's what the orb is missing here.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:55.620
Like, yes, pixel-wise, it gets smaller when it's behind all the objects.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.380
And it then, you know, grows in size as it wraps around.

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:11.800
But it's a little, like, if you gamify it a little bit, like, if you get good at this game, you almost have to start bending it, you know, bending it inwards, right?

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:16.280
To angle it back, to come back closer to the player.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.260
The orb, to do that, you want to do it before it approaches the object that you want to hit.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:33.980
So, you have to think about it like a giant oval, and as you're pulling the gravity back in, you want to get it right before it hits that object, not while it's under that object.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:49.500
Yeah, you've got to start thinking about, like, vectors and ellipses and lots of mechanics like that, which are a lot more intuitive than how we're describing it.

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:56.620
Just because, again, this is a very, we've never had to talk about this kind of mechanic before.

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:04.860
Does the energy system, you know, like the fuel, or whatever you want to call it, I guess it must be fuel.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:09.340
It is fuel. Does that add tension or just make the game feel more punishing?

00:13:10.020 --> 00:13:12.420
I think it does, and I think it's necessary.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:29.120
Um, because one thing that we didn't mention before is that after certain targets are hit, they will release, uh, this kind of, like, thing you don't want to interact with with your ball.

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:33.060
It's like a spiky thing, and if it touches your ball, you lose a lot of fuel.

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:41.340
So I think it did need something to, like, not just have this be a, you have unlimited time.

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:52.900
I think it would be a lot more boring, uh, and you would just have to, it would just be a war of attrition with this game of just, like, would you get bored before you beat it?

00:13:53.820 --> 00:14:09.440
Right, right. Yeah, because also then think about it, if they didn't have the fuel system, the other thing they could have done was treat it like breakout, where you only have, like, three orbs, and if you miss the orb with the paddles, you get two more chances, but once you miss three, then you're out.

00:14:09.600 --> 00:14:16.800
And that's, like, that's not, like, at least the fuel thing is, like, well, you're gonna lose a little more fuel now if you bounce out, but you're not, like, out of it.

00:14:16.800 --> 00:14:26.460
And if they didn't contextualize it with fuel, how would we get, um, the iconic, like, what did they call it?

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:30.060
There's, like, a gas station that you can go to when you press select.

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:32.240
Oh, yeah, that's right.

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:34.580
And then that's almost, like, its own level.

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:40.340
Yeah, it's a very, it's much more difficult than, uh, than the game itself.

00:14:40.340 --> 00:14:53.200
Like, you have to be pixel perfect, threading the needle into these little holes, and every time you get the ball into the hole, you get an amount of fuel back.

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:57.120
And then if you press select again, you can go back to the actual game.

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.640
And I really don't know.

00:14:59.860 --> 00:15:06.520
Like, it's a very strange mechanic, especially because for some people, it could just be completely hidden.

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:08.060
Yes, that's true, right.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:16.340
It is hidden behind, uh, you know, uh, go, pressing that specific button, but also, so the gas station was named, uh, Vern's.

00:15:17.020 --> 00:15:19.600
Vern's, yeah. It's not iconic, but it's like, all right, that's interesting.

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:21.320
No, it's iconic to us.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.560
Yeah, sure. It's weird that they named it at all.

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:31.440
Uh, but also what's weird is, like, you have to go to one of the, um, fueling bays in order to get new fuel.

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:41.860
But, like, there's a chance that none of them are open, and so you have to hit the, um, the dollar sign icon, uh, at the bottom of Vern's service station to open up the fueling bays.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.680
But it's like, you don't collect money in the game.

00:15:45.420 --> 00:15:48.180
Well, there's also, like, bathroom signs.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:53.000
So, I don't think there's a lot of rules with the visual language here.

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.040
And if you hit the bathroom sign, your orb gets flushed down the toilet.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:15:57.200
Is that true?

00:15:57.480 --> 00:15:59.120
No, that's not true. Oh, okay.

00:15:59.800 --> 00:16:24.220
Okay. So, obviously, you know, the UI, it's minimal, um, but it's...

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:25.820
Yeah, but it's functional.

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:31.080
Sure. Everything that's on screen, like, let me put it this way.

00:16:31.200 --> 00:16:34.740
You don't need to read the manual to understand this game.

00:16:34.920 --> 00:16:41.940
Maybe if you're, like, a little slow, you would not be able to understand why the pins, like, keep resetting.

00:16:42.060 --> 00:16:43.640
You might be like, well, what do I have to do?

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:44.920
It's a little slow.

00:16:45.940 --> 00:16:46.980
You know what I mean, though?

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:49.580
It's like, the concept of the game is...

00:16:49.580 --> 00:16:51.020
It's all communicated clearly.

00:16:51.280 --> 00:16:53.740
Oh, yeah. It's all intuitive tasks.

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:59.740
Like, when you get numbers that are jumbled up, it is clear that your task is to put them in order.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:11.160
Like, it's, again, like WarioWare in that way, where you take half a second to internalize the concept that they're playing with in this level,

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:18.040
and then you just do it, it's just not quite as, uh, not quite as easy as WarioWare.

00:17:18.040 --> 00:17:25.320
And if we're being honest, this is the kind of weird experiment on the NES that we're usually here for on the podcast, right?

00:17:25.960 --> 00:17:32.900
Absolutely. It's just that that's what makes the show interesting, is when we play weird games, but that doesn't mean that, like, we're gonna like them.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:34.680
Yeah, I think, like, I did...

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:35.860
I did...

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:45.840
I did think it was neat, um, but I don't think that weird is shorthand for I'm gonna love it.

00:17:47.020 --> 00:17:50.700
And would you really trust the galaxy to be saved by a bouncing ball?

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:56.380
Especially not if it's a bouncing ball they're using to take down an intergalactic wizard.

00:17:56.380 --> 00:17:58.020
Right, what's going on here?

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:08.520
Like, did the, did the civilization or whatever that came up with this, like, plan, did they not figure out space travel, but they figured out, like, traveling by paddles?

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:19.500
Maybe, maybe this is all just in your, like, in your computer heads-up display, like, this is how you navigate, uh, interstellar space.

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:21.040
Could this be a...

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.100
We don't know. We don't.

00:18:23.700 --> 00:18:25.500
Could this be a zen game?

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.840
A zen game.

00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:36.740
You know, like, if you're good at it, like, could you reach, like, uh, a zen-like state of just, uh, you know, this is actually, this game actually calms me down when I play it.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:38.480
I, I guess.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:49.000
I mean, I think that there's a high enough skill ceiling in just being able to manipulate the ball, like, perfectly.

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:52.180
Right, when the ball curves just right.

00:18:52.460 --> 00:18:54.700
Yeah, like, maybe it could get to that point.

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.880
I was, I would have never thought of that.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:18:58.740
That's an interesting question.

00:18:59.000 --> 00:19:06.900
So you don't think kids in the 90s were renting this at Blockbuster and being like, yeah, this is exactly what I wanted out of a space game.

00:19:06.900 --> 00:19:07.880
Oh, wow.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:18.640
I guess maybe it is, like, the, the cover, I know it, I don't, I wouldn't say it looks like a space game, but it definitely looks like a sci-fi game.

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.800
I guess I don't know if I would have thought it was a space game.

00:19:22.300 --> 00:19:27.520
Right, you know, like, you might have thought you were getting another, uh, shmup, but a 3D shmup this time.

00:19:27.760 --> 00:19:30.340
And instead you get 3D Breakout.

00:19:32.660 --> 00:19:43.040
And last but not least, Sean, if this game came out now on Steam, cute little indie title with neon colors and a synthwave soundtrack, would it be a cult hit?

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:45.360
Oh, if it has synthwave soundtrack?

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.180
And neon colors everywhere and, like, future retro?

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:51.200
You could, like, move ducks around.

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:58.920
But it would be like, yeah, but it would be like, um, you know, Mazda Miatas instead.

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:01.480
No, it would have to be ducks.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:02.720
I think I would want a duck.

00:20:02.880 --> 00:20:05.080
Okay, they're ducks, but they're, like, neon ducks.

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:06.360
Oh, yeah, for sure.

00:20:06.400 --> 00:20:12.020
I love neon ducks. Yeah, and it's like, it's all, like, secretly takes place in, like, the back rooms of a Miami bar.

00:20:12.460 --> 00:20:13.280
Okay, for sure.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:14.940
No, I'd be, I'd be down for that.

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:18.160
Great. So, uh, that's what you guys need to do.

00:20:18.160 --> 00:20:21.360
Anybody out there making a video game right now, cancel that game.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:22.920
It's probably too expensive anyway.

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:25.940
And make Orb 3D 2.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:27.620
Orb 2D.

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:29.220
Make Orb 2D. Miami Vice Edition.

00:20:29.480 --> 00:20:31.020
Isn't it weird they went right to 3D?

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.240
We didn't even get Orb 2D.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:34.400
We didn't even get Orb 1D, dude.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:39.260
I'm still impressed you played Expedition 33 without playing the first 32 entries.

00:20:39.860 --> 00:20:41.460
Shut the fuck up.

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:47.960
I might shut up, but not before we do the Essential Games list.

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:55.060
The funny thing about Orb 3D is that I respect it.

00:20:55.440 --> 00:21:02.480
I didn't enjoy it when I was playing it, and it does make it feel, like, I do a few times on this podcast.

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:07.080
It makes it feel like I'm just doing, like, QA testing for nobody.

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:12.300
Like, when I play some of these games, it's like, I'm just making sure that they work.

00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:14.300
And, like, yep, that's the game.

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:17.080
And, like, cool. Time to go review it on a podcast.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:20.360
Like, that's what wound up happening here with Orb 3D.

00:21:20.360 --> 00:21:27.320
I just felt like I was reporting into nobody to report that, yep, this game can release.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:28.560
And it did.

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:33.860
And people bought it numerous times because it's ported onto a bunch of different things.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:39.060
But it's not the kind of game that I'm ever going to seek out again.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:42.800
Or probably this could be one that I wind up forgetting, too.

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:46.460
Because while it is something unique, it's not necessarily memorable.

00:21:47.240 --> 00:21:49.380
So Orb 3D is getting a no from me.

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:52.560
What about you, Sean? Yeah, it's not essential.

00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:58.400
But I will say that I think that the developers of this game are super clever.

00:21:58.740 --> 00:22:03.160
Like, the fact that each level is its own idea.

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:07.160
And the mechanics kind of, the mechanics stay the same.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:12.800
But the rules change every time you boot it up, basically.

00:22:14.080 --> 00:22:15.940
I think that's really cool.

00:22:15.940 --> 00:22:21.440
I think maybe, I do wonder what they made later.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:26.980
If we're going to play another game by them that maybe isn't as super high concept.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:31.360
But still allowed this kind of creativity again.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:35.740
But as of right now, it's just not super fun.

00:22:36.120 --> 00:22:38.020
But it's impressive.

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:42.180
I welcome, you know, like, Orb 4D.

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:45.740
Which probably came out on, like, Atari Jaguar or something.

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:49.320
You know, like, just something where it's like, yeah, we don't have 4D.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:51.540
Just hypercubes everywhere. Yeah, but we just don't have it.

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:54.420
We're just going to shamelessly call it 4D.

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:57.460
But nobody really cares if it is or isn't.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:03.740
Because Orb 4D, you know, I'm not up to date on the latest, like, what dimensions or what.

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:08.220
But, like, that would be, like, you would just see everywhere the Orb ever was and will be.

00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:10.760
I think...

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:12.120
Because it's time, right?

00:23:12.120 --> 00:23:15.020
And sometimes people say that it's time.

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:17.600
But it could also be, like, a spatial dimension.

00:23:17.620 --> 00:23:19.620
And that's where you get hypercubes from.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:21.380
Oh, right. The hypercube.

00:23:21.500 --> 00:23:26.380
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Yeah. I'm not a quantum mechanist.

00:23:26.700 --> 00:23:55.020
I'm not a quantum machinist. I'm not a quantum mechanist.
